The scrolling thread moves the page while the main thread is busy.

With a wheel event handled on the scrolling thread (patch 0029 makes that
the case for pages whose wheel listeners are passive), the scroll position
changes there, but the layers move only when one of two things happens: the
main thread runs a rendering update, or the scrolling thread decides that
the main thread is too slow and moves them itself
(ThreadedScrollingTree::displayDidRefreshOnScrollingThread()).  The second
needs two things the main thread provides, and a busy main thread provides
neither:

  - It starts only once a rendering update has been asked for
    (m_renderingUpdateWasScheduled).  After a scroll the main thread asks
    when it gets to the task the scrolling thread posts for it; a script
    that holds it for a second holds that request for a second.  Animated
    scrolls set the flag from the scrolling thread already
    (ScrollingTreeCoordinated::hasNodeWithAnimatedScrollChanged); plain
    ones -- luakit turns smooth scrolling off by default -- did not.  The
    scrolling thread now sets it when it scrolls.

  - It runs on display refreshes.  The UI process sends those to the web
    process's event dispatcher (off its main thread) while the user scrolls
    with the wheel, but only to a process that also has a display refresh
    observer registered, which the main thread registers -- for its own
    rendering updates, or when an animated scroll starts.  A page that was
    idle when the wheel started had none, and the display link may not even
    have been running.  A process that asks for full-speed updates now gets
    them with no observer, and the request starts the display link.

Measured with this engine's upstream build on a desktop host, threaded
scrolling on, wheel events handled on the scrolling thread, microsoft.com,
a 1.5 s script on the main thread: with a requestAnimationFrame loop running
on the page the view kept scrolling through the script; without one it
stopped for 1.48 s (smooth scrolling off) and 1.49 s (on).

